## Why This Is Asked
This question tests building an observability-first, Azure-native pipeline with tenant isolation and simple DR, a common beginner-to-intermediate pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Event Hubs, Log Analytics, Azure Monitor, Application Insights
- Delta Lake on ADLS Gen2, per-tenant RBAC
- BYOK via Key Vault, basic DR to paired region
- Cost levers: retention, sampling, tiered storage

## Code Example
```javascript
// publish per-tenant logs to Event Hubs
const { EventHubProducerClient } = require('@azure/event-hubs');
const client = new EventHubProducerClient('<conn>', '<hub>');
async function send(tenantId, payload){
  const batch = await client.createBatch({ partitionKey: tenantId });
  batch.tryAdd(JSON.stringify({ tenantId, payload }));
  await client.sendBatch(batch);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you enforce per-tenant RBAC across API, storage, and analytics views?
- What retention and cost controls would you apply to logs and metrics?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to design scalable, secure, cost-aware multi-tenant inference pipelines with strict data residency. Tests model management (canary deployments, drift detection), data governance (Purview/RBAC), and cross-region DR. Realistic latency targets and tenant isolation are central.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant isolation (per-tenant namespaces and feature stores)
- Real-time latency targets (<100ms p99)
- Model lifecycle: registry, canary deployments, drift monitoring
- Data governance: Purview RBAC lineage, BYOK encryption in rest/transit
- Cost optimization: autoscaling, per-tenant quotas, reserved capacity

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo routing for tenant-aware inference (high level)
function routeRequest(req) {
  const tenant = extractTenant(req);
  const endpoint = getTenantEndpoint(tenant);
  return callModelEndpoint(endpoint, req.payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test canary deployments at scale?
- How would you handle tenant onboarding/offboarding and data deletion?

